{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3054","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3054","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3054\",\"parent_revision\":\"8b4db7361f00bad57528801786251fbf285c41c3\",\"root_revision\":\"b97b5be773066ecc2e71fc6346de923c524d249e13419a910610965c14ce98fe\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe plugin's documentation does not describe its own structure. There is no component map. Eight code packages exist (`agent_profile`, `backlog_core`, `dh_core`, `dispatch_schema`, `hooks`, `progressive_markdown`, `sam_schema`, `scripts`); exactly one of them has an `ARCHITECTURE.md`. Nothing states what each package owns, what it consumes, which packages are near-duplicates of each other, or where the authoritative detail for each lives.\\n\\nThe consequence is measurable and was demonstrated repeatedly in a single session: an agent starting work reads whichever package it lands in, treats that package's behaviour as the system's behaviour, and generalises incorrectly. Specific failures observed, each traceable to a missing line of a component map:\\n\\n- The markdown engine (`progressive_markdown`) was not discovered until late, by accident, after a search in a sibling directory failed. Work was nearly started to build a capability that already existed and was fully tested.\\n- Two distinct markdown handling paths \u2014 storage-key resolution and navigation indexing \u2014 were conflated, producing a confident and wrong claim that the system handled only two heading levels.\\n- A duplicate test file was created because only one of the plugin's six test roots was searched.\\n- A new architecture document was committed with no deduplication against two sibling documents it overlapped, no cross-references, and no entry in any discovery index.\\n- Two committed documents were found to state directly contradictory behaviour, discovered only incidentally.\\n\\nThis item covers the systematic pass. It is deliberately sequenced after the targeted markdown-consumption architecture work so that the smaller, well-understood surface is settled first and can serve as the pattern.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T04:21:27.180727Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: docs: per-module architecture refresh, concept consolidation, and documentation-versus-behaviour drift audit\\nDate: 2026-08-20\\nGoal: Determine whether this item is ready to plan given progress made on its own stated prerequisite since it was filed.\\nConditions:\\n1. Item's own stated prerequisite (targeted markdown-consumption architecture description) is settled | Status: AVAILABLE (verified false \u2014 PR #3080 is open, unmerged, three review rounds addressed but not yet merged)\\n2. Whether all 8 code packages currently have at least summary-level architecture coverage | Status: AVAILABLE (verified true for a package-level summary \u2014 component-architecture.md, added this session, covers all 8; per-package depth beyond that summary is what this item's Deliverable 1 still requires)\\n3. Whether a concept glossary exists anywhere in the plugin | Status: AVAILABLE (verified true for one domain only \u2014 CONTEXT.md + root CONTEXT-MAP.md, added this session, cover markdown consumption; no glossary exists for the other 7 packages)\\n4. Shared root path and naming convention for per-module architecture docs across all 8 packages | Status: MISSING \u2014 no decision has been made on whether CONTEXT.md/CONTEXT-MAP.md's pattern (established this session for one domain) is the convention to replicate plugin-wide, or something else\\n5. Documentation-versus-behaviour drift audit status outside the markdown-consumption area | Status: AVAILABLE (verified: not started \u2014 only the markdown-consumption docs were audited this session)\\n6. Cross-reference/duplicate-source table for the whole plugin | Status: AVAILABLE (verified: does not exist)\\nAVAILABLE count: 4\\nDERIVABLE count: 0\\nMISSING count: 1\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T06:05:39.448548Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n\\n**Rationale**: The five distinct failures documented (late engine discovery, conflated paths, duplicate test file, uncoordinated docs, contradictory docs) are not caused by broken code but by the absence of documentation structure\u2014specifically a component map, naming conventions, and discovery index. The system works but lacks guardrails (structural conventions, cross-references, indexes) that should prevent agent confusion about module boundaries and coordination gaps. This is a design-gap classification, not a defect.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: N/A \u2014 missing-guardrail type requires no root-cause analysis.\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Agents starting work on development-harness read one isolated module, treat its behavior as system behavior, and generalize incorrectly, producing confusion about boundaries, duplicate work, and contradictory docs (five instances observed) \u2192 After implementing component map, per-package architecture docs, shared naming convention, discovery index, and cross-reference table, agents navigate the 8-package structure correctly and avoid module confusion and coordination gaps.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T04:31:14.662662Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-08-20T04:34:13.202116Z \u2014 Correct provenance claim about CONTEXT-MAP.md (committed on PR #3080's branch, not uncommitted; earlier git log check used a broken glob pathspec)</summary>\\n\\n### Research \u2014 development-harness per-module docs: shared root path, naming convention, drift-audit methodology \u2014 2026-08-20\\n\\n**Research method**: This ran as a single agent, not four independent parallel angles. This\\nsession's toolset had no `WebSearch`, `Ref`, or `context7` MCP tools despite the loaded\\napi-state/ecosystem-research skill instructions naming them \u2014 external OSS convention surveys\\nwere not possible. All findings below come from reading actual files in this repo (git history,\\n`gh pr view`, direct file reads) plus one installed skill's own SKILL.md. No `[CROSS-ANGLE]`\\nmarkers are used \u2014 there was no second independent pass to corroborate against. Every claim below\\ncites its file path (and commit/PR where relevant).\\n\\n### Codebase Audit \u2014 existing conventions (three, already in tension, pre-dating this research)\\n\\n**(a) Package-aligned deep-detail doc \u2014 `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` (63,120 chars).**\\nPredates this session (created in commit `89ab8b2`, 2026-03-01, per its own historical note and\\n`backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md`). It is the only one of the 8 packages with this file.\\n`docs/component-architecture.md` already cross-links it as the \\\"Detail:\\\" target for `backlog_core`.\\nThis is a working, referenced precedent: per-package deep detail lives at that package's own root.\\n\\n**(b) Root plugin-wide summary \u2014 `docs/component-architecture.md` (8,189 chars).**\\nAdded this session (commit `87a3fd178`, PR #3056). Covers all 8 packages at one-paragraph depth\\neach, with explicit \\\"Do not confuse with\\\" distinction notes and \\\"Detail:\\\" links out to per-package\\ndocs. Its own \\\"Documentation layout\\\" section already states a partial answer to Deliverable 3:\\n\\\"Documents live in this directory and are indexed by the Required Reading table in the plugin's\\n`AGENTS.md`. A document that no index references is unreachable... check whether an existing\\ndocument already covers the topic.\\\" `AGENTS.md`'s \\\"Required Reading by Task Type\\\" table (from\\nline ~402) is confirmed as the actual discovery index in current use \u2014 every doc added this\\nsession is already listed there under \\\"Modifying markdown consumption...\\\".\\n\\n**(c) Root domain glossary \u2014 `CONTEXT.md` (3,600 chars) + `CONTEXT-MAP.md`.**\\n`CONTEXT.md` sits at the plugin root (`plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md`), added this\\nsession, and is *not* per-package \u2014 it covers the \\\"markdown consumption\\\" domain, which spans three\\npackages (`progressive_markdown`, `backlog_core`, `sam_schema`).\\n`CONTEXT-MAP.md` does **not** exist in the working tree or in `git log --all` history. It exists\\nonly as an unmerged file in open PR #3080 (\\\"docs(development-harness): resolve the contract's five\\nopen decisions with ADRs\\\", branch `docs/markdown-engine-decisions`), confirmed via\\n`gh pr view 3080 -R Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills --json files`. Critically, that file is placed\\nat the **monorepo repo root** (`/CONTEXT-MAP.md`), not the plugin root\\n(`plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT-MAP.md`) \u2014 read directly from the working tree at\\n`/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/CONTEXT-MAP.md` (currently on this branch as an uncommitted\\ncarry-forward), content is a single \\\"Contexts\\\" entry pointing to\\n`./plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md`, plus an empty \\\"Relationships\\\" placeholder. If\\n`plugins/development-harness/` is ever zipped and installed standalone (the self-containment\\nconstraint documented in `plugins/development-harness/docs/plugin-deployment-model.md`), a\\nrepo-root map file does not travel with the plugin bundle and orphans the plugin's own `CONTEXT.md`\\nfrom its index \u2014 the exact \\\"unreachable document\\\" failure mode `docs/component-architecture.md`'s\\nown layout policy warns against, self-inflicted on the artifact meant to prevent it.\\n\\n**(d) ADRs \u2014 `docs/adrs/ADR-{issue}-{n}-slug.md` (6 files), plus one outlier.**\\nPre-existing, plugin-root, issue-number-keyed \u2014 already a deliberate deviation from the generic\\ndomain-modeling skill's default `docs/adr/NNNN-slug.md` sequential numbering (see below). One\\nexisting file, `docs/adr-9-close-resolve-semantics.md`, sits directly in `docs/` with neither the\\n`adrs/` subdirectory nor the `ADR-{issue}-{n}` naming \u2014 a third, older naming variant. Referenced\\nby related item #3050 (\\\"ADR identifiers referenced but undefined \u2014 a subset of the same problem\\\").\\n\\n### Codebase Audit \u2014 the generic convention this session drew from\\n\\nRead directly from the installed skill `mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling`'s SKILL.md\\n(`~/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/mattpocock-skills/1.2.3/skills/engineering/domain-modeling/SKILL.md`).\\nIts prescribed file structure:\\n\\n- Single-context repos: root `CONTEXT.md` + root `docs/adr/NNNN-slug.md`.\\n- Multi-context repos: root `CONTEXT-MAP.md` pointing to each context, where **each context is a\\n  `src/{context}/` subdirectory** containing its own `CONTEXT.md` and `docs/adr/`; system-wide\\n  ADRs stay at the repo root's `docs/adr/`.\\n\\nThe skill's own file-structure example equates \\\"a context\\\" with \\\"a directory\\\" 1:1 \u2014 it assumes\\ndomain boundaries align with source-directory boundaries.\\n\\n### The discriminating tension (why this is a real design decision, not a lookup)\\n\\nThis plugin's actual domains do not align 1:1 with its 8 code packages. The one domain modeled so\\nfar (\\\"markdown consumption\\\") spans 3 packages. Applying the generic skill's per-directory-context\\npattern literally would fragment that vocabulary into 3 separate per-package `CONTEXT.md` files\\nwith no single owner for shared terms (e.g. \\\"Provider\\\", used identically across all three) \u2014\\ndirectly violating this item's Deliverable 2 (\\\"one definition per concept, stated once... every\\nother document links to the definition rather than restating it\\\"). This session's actual choice \u2014\\none root `CONTEXT.md` per cross-cutting *domain*, not per package \u2014 already deviates from the\\ngeneric skill's literal example, for exactly this reason, independent of whether that deviation\\nwas made deliberately.\\n\\nBy contrast, Deliverable 1 (per-package role/owns/consumes/consumed-by/detail-location) aligns\\nnaturally with package boundaries \u2014 this is already how `docs/component-architecture.md` (summary)\\n+ per-package `ARCHITECTURE.md` (detail, precedent (a) above) work today for `backlog_core`. A\\nsingle directory-unit convention that forces both deliverables onto the same unit (e.g. always\\n`<package>/CONTEXT.md`) will misfit whichever of the two deliverables does not naturally align with\\nthat unit. Evidence supports treating architecture-detail placement (package-aligned) and glossary\\nplacement (domain-aligned, which may span packages) as two different axes, not one shared rule \u2014\\nthis is the design decision RT-ICA condition 4 flags as MISSING; this note surfaces the evidence,\\nit does not choose between the axes for the human/rtica-assessor.\\n\\n### Quantitative Impact\\n\\nBaseline (measured via `wc -c`/`find`, 2026-08-20, `plugins/development-harness/`):\\n\\n| Artifact | Size (chars) | Est. tokens (\u00f73.5) | Source |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| All `.md` files in plugin (excl. `__pycache__`) | \u2014 | \u2014 | `find . -iname \\\"*.md\\\"` \u2192 244 files |\\n| `skills/*/SKILL.md` (agent-facing prose, in scope per Deliverable 4's \\\"prose instruction\\\") | \u2014 | \u2014 | `find skills -iname SKILL.md` \u2192 59 files |\\n| `docs/*.md` (root docs, non-recursive) | 393,717 | ~112,491 | `find docs -maxdepth 1 -iname \\\"*.md\\\" -exec wc -c` \u2192 25 files |\\n| `docs/adrs/*.md` | \u2014 | \u2014 | 6 files |\\n| `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` (per-package detail precedent) | 63,120 | ~18,034 | `wc -c` |\\n| `backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` (per-package drift-audit precedent) | 28,002 | ~8,001 | `wc -c` |\\n| `CONTEXT.md` (1 domain, spans 3 packages) | 3,600 | ~1,029 | `wc -c` |\\n| `docs/component-architecture.md` (all-8-package summary) | 8,189 | ~2,340 | `wc -c` |\\n\\nContext injection cost tier (per this skill's tiering): `docs/component-architecture.md` at\\n~2,340 tokens = Notable. `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` at ~18,034 tokens = Blocking risk if ever\\nloaded whole rather than navigated/paginated. This is direct evidence for why the existing\\nconvention keeps the root summary small and defers depth to per-package files reached via a link,\\nrather than inlining detail at the root: the per-package detail file is already large enough on\\nits own to justify treating it as a separate document rather than a section of the summary.\\n\\nDelta / scale projection (pre-implementation estimate, explicitly rough): if `backlog_core`'s\\nARCHITECTURE.md depth is used as a per-package benchmark and replicated across the remaining 7\\npackages, that is a rough order-of-magnitude estimate of ~7\u00d7 63,120 \u2248 441,840 additional chars\\n(~126,240 tokens) of new per-package detail \u2014 flagged as a rough scale signal, not a real\\nprojection, since the 7 remaining packages vary widely in size (`hooks` and `agent_profile` are\\nfar smaller than `backlog_core`; `sam_schema` and `dispatch_schema` have `core/readers/writers`\\nsubdirectory structure that may warrant sub-documents).\\n\\nFor the drift audit specifically: `backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` covered 1 doc\\n(its own `ARCHITECTURE.md`) against 6 implementation modules and produced 24 findings (3 critical\\nmismatches, 12 implemented-but-undocumented, 3 documented-but-unimplemented, 9 outdated/mismatched\\ndetails \u2014 see its \\\"Executive Summary\\\"). Item #3054's Deliverable 4 scope is the whole plugin:\\nalready ~31 known documents (25 root `docs/*.md` + 6 ADRs, before Deliverable 1-3 even add new\\nper-package files) against the full 8-package implementation tree, plus the 59 `SKILL.md` prose\\nfiles the item's \\\"code, workflow, and prose instruction alike\\\" language explicitly brings into\\nscope \u2014 a scope multiplier on the order of 25-30\u00d7 the one-package precedent by document count\\nalone.\\n\\nNo published token-cost or drift-audit-runtime benchmarks were found beyond the artifacts measured\\ndirectly above (searched: `docs/`, package roots, `README.md` files \u2014 none contain benchmark data).\\n\\n### Codebase Audit \u2014 drift-audit methodology precedents (two, at different rigor levels)\\n\\n**Precedent 1 (lighter, package-scoped): `backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md`.**\\nMethod, reconstructed from the file itself: pick one spec doc + its implementing modules; use\\n`git log`/blame on both to build a timeline correlating which commit touched code vs. doc; walk\\nthe implementation and classify each finding into one of 4 *drift-type* categories (Implemented\\nbut Undocumented / Documented but Unimplemented / Outdated Documentation / Mismatched Details);\\neach finding records: doc location + claim, code location + reality, and a recommendation;\\nfindings are then triaged into a flat Critical/High/Medium/Low priority list. Sample finding\\n(FIND-1) confirms the per-entry shape: doc claim (with line ref), code reality (with line ref and\\nsnippet), single recommendation \u2014 no adjudication field distinguishing \\\"doc wrong\\\" from \\\"code\\nwrong\\\" from \\\"neither is right.\\\" This is a genuine, working precedent, but it categorizes *what kind*\\nof drift occurred, not *which side should change* \u2014 item #3054's Deliverable 4 explicitly requires\\nthe latter (three adjudication outcomes: doc correct/impl must change; impl correct/doc must\\nchange; neither correct/new direction supplied). Precedent 1's shape needs an added adjudication\\naxis to satisfy Deliverable 4 as written, not to be discarded.\\n\\n**Precedent 2 (heavier, plugin-wide, closer fit): `docs/audits/purpose-audit-*.yaml` three-file\\npipeline** (`purpose-audit-reference-manifest.yaml`, `purpose-audit-clause-evidence-manifest.yaml`,\\n`purpose-audit-remediation-plan.yaml`), auditing `docs/PURPOSE.md` against the whole plugin at\\nbaseline commits `2a58fda0`/`2c76f47d`, all marked `frozen: true`. Structure:\\n\\n1. **Reference-selection manifest**: declares a `source_precedence` order (target contract clauses\\n   \u2192 current-boundary clauses \u2192 executable protocols/config/transport/provider implementations \u2192\\n   published plugin/lifecycle/skill contracts \u2192 focused tests \u2192 supporting ADRs/procedures), an\\n   explicit include/exclude selection rule, and an `audit_safety` block (`mode: read-only`,\\n   isolated backend defaults) \u2014 this fixes what counts as evidence and how the audit avoids\\n   mutating real state before any finding is produced.\\n2. **Clause/evidence manifest**: atomically maps every clause of the target doc to selected\\n   sources, with an explicit `evidence_schema` classifying each assertion as `documented-claim`\\n   (what the system declares) / `implementation-fact` (a structural property observable in\\n   committed source) / `test-evidence` (an executable test's assertion) / `confidence`. This is\\n   directly a per-claim fact-check method matching Deliverable 4's per-discrepancy record\\n   requirement.\\n3. **Remediation plan**: dependency-ordered tasks derived from the partitioned (A-D) audit\\n   findings, with a `decision_log` of resolved user decisions (each entry: id, date, clause_ids,\\n   origin) that closes a \\\"needs user decision\\\" flag raised by a partition auditor \u2014 this is\\n   functionally the three-outcome adjudication mechanism Deliverable 4 asks for, already\\n   implemented and used at plugin scale (not package scale) in this repo.\\n\\nPrecedent 2 is the closer methodological fit for #3054's Deliverable 4 scope (whole plugin,\\nadjudicated outcomes) than Precedent 1 (package-scoped, drift-type-only). A related process audit,\\n`docs/process-audit-backlog-lifecycle-2026-03-02.md` (2026-03-02, method: `/improve-processes`\\nexcellence checklist + triage protocol), is a third precedent but targets workflow-process quality\\nagainst a checklist rubric, not document-vs-code fact-checking \u2014 relevant to the \\\"workflow\\\" leg of\\nDeliverable 4's \\\"code, workflow, and prose instruction alike\\\" but not a drift-audit method on its\\nown.\\n\\n### Ecosystem and Compatibility \u2014 Gaps (tool unavailability)\\n\\nNo `WebSearch`, `Ref`, or `context7` MCP tools were present in this agent's available toolset for\\nthis task, despite being named in the loaded api-state/ecosystem-research skill instructions. Per\\nthis repo's own operating expectation (\\\"technology may name an internal module... external angles\\nwill report short Gaps sections, which is expected\\\"), no external OSS convention survey (e.g. how\\nother multi-plugin/monorepo agent-tooling projects structure per-module docs) was performed. The\\nonly external input available \u2014 the installed `mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling` skill's own\\nSKILL.md \u2014 was read directly and is cited in full above; treat it as the entire \\\"ecosystem\\\" input\\nto this note, not a survey.\\n\\n### Conflicts\\n\\n[CONFLICT] RT-ICA condition 3 on this item's own body states: \\\"CONTEXT.md + root CONTEXT-MAP.md,\\nadded this session.\\\" Verified state at research time: `CONTEXT-MAP.md` does not exist in the\\nmerged working tree or in `git log --all` history; it exists only as an unmerged file in open PR\\n#3080, and it is placed at the monorepo root, not the plugin root \u2014 a distinction the RT-ICA\\nsnapshot's phrasing does not disambiguate.\\n[RESOLVED \u2014 this is a single verifiable fact, not a disagreement between independent research\\nangles]: `CONTEXT-MAP.md` exists only as an unmerged diff in PR #3080, at path `/CONTEXT-MAP.md`\\n(repo root), containing exactly one context entry pointing to\\n`./plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md`. This is itself a live instance of the documentation\\ndrift #3054 targets \u2014 RT-ICA condition 3's wording should be corrected at the next grooming pass\\nto state the file is unmerged and mis-rooted, not \\\"added this session\\\" without qualification.\\n\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\n\\nNone applicable \u2014 this was a single-agent pass with no independent second angle to cross-check\\nagainst (tool unavailability, see Ecosystem section above).\\n\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\n\\n- No external convention survey beyond the one locally-installed skill (`WebSearch`/`Ref`/\\n  `context7` unavailable in this session's toolset).\\n- Runtime/dynamic drift (behaviour only observable by executing code \u2014 e.g. actual FastMCP tool\\n  response shapes vs. documented schemas) was not measured; this note is static-analysis only,\\n  consistent with the codebase-auditor angle's operating constraints.\\n- The 244 `.md` files in the plugin were not individually checked for drift status \u2014 that\\n  execution is Deliverable 4 itself, out of scope for this research pass, which targets RT-ICA\\n  condition 4 (shared root path / naming convention) and drift-audit methodology only.\\n- The `docs/audits/purpose-audit-*.yaml` pipeline's completion/adoption status beyond its frozen\\n  baseline headers (dated 2026-07-26, commits `2a58fda0`/`2c76f47d`) was not traced further \u2014\\n  worth a direct read of `purpose-audit-remediation-plan.yaml`'s full `decision_log` and task list\\n  before Deliverable 4 execution begins, to confirm whether its remediation tasks were carried out\\n  and whether its manifest schema is reusable as-is or needs adaptation for a code-vs-doc (not\\n  purpose-doc-vs-code) audit target.\\n\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-20T04:34:13.202170Z</sub>\\n\\n### Research \u2014 development-harness per-module docs: shared root path, naming convention, drift-audit methodology \u2014 2026-08-20\\n\\n**Research method**: This ran as a single agent, not four independent parallel angles. This\\nsession's toolset had no `WebSearch`, `Ref`, or `context7` MCP tools despite the loaded\\napi-state/ecosystem-research skill instructions naming them \u2014 external OSS convention surveys\\nwere not possible. All findings below come from reading actual files in this repo (git history,\\n`gh pr view`, direct file reads) plus one installed skill's own SKILL.md. No `[CROSS-ANGLE]`\\nmarkers are used \u2014 there was no second independent pass to corroborate against. Every claim below\\ncites its file path (and commit/PR where relevant).\\n\\n### Codebase Audit \u2014 existing conventions (three, already in tension, pre-dating this research)\\n\\n**(a) Package-aligned deep-detail doc \u2014 `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` (63,120 chars).**\\nPredates this session (created in commit `89ab8b2`, 2026-03-01, per its own historical note and\\n`backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md`). It is the only one of the 8 packages with this file.\\n`docs/component-architecture.md` already cross-links it as the \\\"Detail:\\\" target for `backlog_core`.\\nThis is a working, referenced precedent: per-package deep detail lives at that package's own root.\\n\\n**(b) Root plugin-wide summary \u2014 `docs/component-architecture.md` (8,189 chars).**\\nAdded this session (commit `87a3fd178`, PR #3056). Covers all 8 packages at one-paragraph depth\\neach, with explicit \\\"Do not confuse with\\\" distinction notes and \\\"Detail:\\\" links out to per-package\\ndocs. Its own \\\"Documentation layout\\\" section already states a partial answer to Deliverable 3:\\n\\\"Documents live in this directory and are indexed by the Required Reading table in the plugin's\\n`AGENTS.md`. A document that no index references is unreachable... check whether an existing\\ndocument already covers the topic.\\\" `AGENTS.md`'s \\\"Required Reading by Task Type\\\" table (from\\nline ~402) is confirmed as the actual discovery index in current use \u2014 every doc added this\\nsession is already listed there under \\\"Modifying markdown consumption...\\\".\\n\\n**(c) Root domain glossary \u2014 `CONTEXT.md` (3,600 chars) + `CONTEXT-MAP.md`.**\\n`CONTEXT.md` sits at the plugin root (`plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md`), added this\\nsession, and is *not* per-package \u2014 it covers the \\\"markdown consumption\\\" domain, which spans three\\npackages (`progressive_markdown`, `backlog_core`, `sam_schema`).\\n`CONTEXT-MAP.md` is committed on open PR #3080's unmerged branch (`docs/markdown-engine-decisions`,\\ncommit `9bcc2bbf0`, \\\"resolve the contract's five open decisions with ADRs\\\") \u2014 confirmed via\\n`gh pr view 3080 -R Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills --json files` and `git log --all --oneline --\\nCONTEXT-MAP.md`. It is absent from `main`. This checkout currently has that PR branch checked out\\n(`git branch --show-current` \u2192 `docs/markdown-engine-decisions`), which is why the file is present\\non disk here. Critically, the file is placed at the **monorepo repo root** (`/CONTEXT-MAP.md`), not\\nthe plugin root (`plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT-MAP.md`) \u2014 read directly at\\n`/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/CONTEXT-MAP.md`, content is a single \\\"Contexts\\\" entry\\npointing to `./plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md`, plus an empty \\\"Relationships\\\" placeholder.\\nIf `plugins/development-harness/` is ever zipped and installed standalone (the self-containment\\nconstraint documented in `plugins/development-harness/docs/plugin-deployment-model.md`), a\\nrepo-root map file does not travel with the plugin bundle and orphans the plugin's own `CONTEXT.md`\\nfrom its index \u2014 the exact \\\"unreachable document\\\" failure mode `docs/component-architecture.md`'s\\nown layout policy warns against, self-inflicted on the artifact meant to prevent it, and currently\\nstill unmerged/unresolved on `main`.\\n\\n**(d) ADRs \u2014 `docs/adrs/ADR-{issue}-{n}-slug.md` (6 files), plus one outlier.**\\nPre-existing, plugin-root, issue-number-keyed \u2014 already a deliberate deviation from the generic\\ndomain-modeling skill's default `docs/adr/NNNN-slug.md` sequential numbering (see below). One\\nexisting file, `docs/adr-9-close-resolve-semantics.md`, sits directly in `docs/` with neither the\\n`adrs/` subdirectory nor the `ADR-{issue}-{n}` naming \u2014 a third, older naming variant. Referenced\\nby related item #3050 (\\\"ADR identifiers referenced but undefined \u2014 a subset of the same problem\\\").\\n\\n### Codebase Audit \u2014 the generic convention this session drew from\\n\\nRead directly from the installed skill `mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling`'s SKILL.md\\n(`~/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/mattpocock-skills/1.2.3/skills/engineering/domain-modeling/SKILL.md`).\\nIts prescribed file structure:\\n\\n- Single-context repos: root `CONTEXT.md` + root `docs/adr/NNNN-slug.md`.\\n- Multi-context repos: root `CONTEXT-MAP.md` pointing to each context, where **each context is a\\n  `src/{context}/` subdirectory** containing its own `CONTEXT.md` and `docs/adr/`; system-wide\\n  ADRs stay at the repo root's `docs/adr/`.\\n\\nThe skill's own file-structure example equates \\\"a context\\\" with \\\"a directory\\\" 1:1 \u2014 it assumes\\ndomain boundaries align with source-directory boundaries.\\n\\n### The discriminating tension (why this is a real design decision, not a lookup)\\n\\nThis plugin's actual domains do not align 1:1 with its 8 code packages. The one domain modeled so\\nfar (\\\"markdown consumption\\\") spans 3 packages. Applying the generic skill's per-directory-context\\npattern literally would fragment that vocabulary into 3 separate per-package `CONTEXT.md` files\\nwith no single owner for shared terms (e.g. \\\"Provider\\\", used identically across all three) \u2014\\ndirectly violating this item's Deliverable 2 (\\\"one definition per concept, stated once... every\\nother document links to the definition rather than restating it\\\"). This session's actual choice \u2014\\none root `CONTEXT.md` per cross-cutting *domain*, not per package \u2014 already deviates from the\\ngeneric skill's literal example, for exactly this reason, independent of whether that deviation\\nwas made deliberately.\\n\\nBy contrast, Deliverable 1 (per-package role/owns/consumes/consumed-by/detail-location) aligns\\nnaturally with package boundaries \u2014 this is already how `docs/component-architecture.md` (summary)\\n+ per-package `ARCHITECTURE.md` (detail, precedent (a) above) work today for `backlog_core`. A\\nsingle directory-unit convention that forces both deliverables onto the same unit (e.g. always\\n`<package>/CONTEXT.md`) will misfit whichever of the two deliverables does not naturally align with\\nthat unit. Evidence supports treating architecture-detail placement (package-aligned) and glossary\\nplacement (domain-aligned, which may span packages) as two different axes, not one shared rule \u2014\\nthis is the design decision RT-ICA condition 4 flags as MISSING; this note surfaces the evidence,\\nit does not choose between the axes for the human/rtica-assessor.\\n\\n### Quantitative Impact\\n\\nBaseline (measured via `wc -c`/`find`, 2026-08-20, `plugins/development-harness/`):\\n\\n| Artifact | Size (chars) | Est. tokens (\u00f73.5) | Source |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| All `.md` files in plugin (excl. `__pycache__`) | \u2014 | \u2014 | `find . -iname \\\"*.md\\\"` \u2192 244 files |\\n| `skills/*/SKILL.md` (agent-facing prose, in scope per Deliverable 4's \\\"prose instruction\\\") | \u2014 | \u2014 | `find skills -iname SKILL.md` \u2192 59 files |\\n| `docs/*.md` (root docs, non-recursive) | 393,717 | ~112,491 | `find docs -maxdepth 1 -iname \\\"*.md\\\" -exec wc -c` \u2192 25 files |\\n| `docs/adrs/*.md` | \u2014 | \u2014 | 6 files |\\n| `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` (per-package detail precedent) | 63,120 | ~18,034 | `wc -c` |\\n| `backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` (per-package drift-audit precedent) | 28,002 | ~8,001 | `wc -c` |\\n| `CONTEXT.md` (1 domain, spans 3 packages) | 3,600 | ~1,029 | `wc -c` |\\n| `docs/component-architecture.md` (all-8-package summary) | 8,189 | ~2,340 | `wc -c` |\\n\\nContext injection cost tier (per this skill's tiering): `docs/component-architecture.md` at\\n~2,340 tokens = Notable. `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` at ~18,034 tokens = Blocking risk if ever\\nloaded whole rather than navigated/paginated. This is direct evidence for why the existing\\nconvention keeps the root summary small and defers depth to per-package files reached via a link,\\nrather than inlining detail at the root: the per-package detail file is already large enough on\\nits own to justify treating it as a separate document rather than a section of the summary.\\n\\nDelta / scale projection (pre-implementation estimate, explicitly rough): if `backlog_core`'s\\nARCHITECTURE.md depth is used as a per-package benchmark and replicated across the remaining 7\\npackages, that is a rough order-of-magnitude estimate of ~7\u00d7 63,120 \u2248 441,840 additional chars\\n(~126,240 tokens) of new per-package detail \u2014 flagged as a rough scale signal, not a real\\nprojection, since the 7 remaining packages vary widely in size (`hooks` and `agent_profile` are\\nfar smaller than `backlog_core`; `sam_schema` and `dispatch_schema` have `core/readers/writers`\\nsubdirectory structure that may warrant sub-documents).\\n\\nFor the drift audit specifically: `backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` covered 1 doc\\n(its own `ARCHITECTURE.md`) against 6 implementation modules and produced 24 findings (3 critical\\nmismatches, 12 implemented-but-undocumented, 3 documented-but-unimplemented, 9 outdated/mismatched\\ndetails \u2014 see its \\\"Executive Summary\\\"). Item #3054's Deliverable 4 scope is the whole plugin:\\nalready ~31 known documents (25 root `docs/*.md` + 6 ADRs, before Deliverable 1-3 even add new\\nper-package files) against the full 8-package implementation tree, plus the 59 `SKILL.md` prose\\nfiles the item's \\\"code, workflow, and prose instruction alike\\\" language explicitly brings into\\nscope \u2014 a scope multiplier on the order of 25-30\u00d7 the one-package precedent by document count\\nalone.\\n\\nNo published token-cost or drift-audit-runtime benchmarks were found beyond the artifacts measured\\ndirectly above (searched: `docs/`, package roots, `README.md` files \u2014 none contain benchmark data).\\n\\n### Codebase Audit \u2014 drift-audit methodology precedents (two, at different rigor levels)\\n\\n**Precedent 1 (lighter, package-scoped): `backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md`.**\\nMethod, reconstructed from the file itself: pick one spec doc + its implementing modules; use\\n`git log`/blame on both to build a timeline correlating which commit touched code vs. doc; walk\\nthe implementation and classify each finding into one of 4 *drift-type* categories (Implemented\\nbut Undocumented / Documented but Unimplemented / Outdated Documentation / Mismatched Details);\\neach finding records: doc location + claim, code location + reality, and a recommendation;\\nfindings are then triaged into a flat Critical/High/Medium/Low priority list. Sample finding\\n(FIND-1) confirms the per-entry shape: doc claim (with line ref), code reality (with line ref and\\nsnippet), single recommendation \u2014 no adjudication field distinguishing \\\"doc wrong\\\" from \\\"code\\nwrong\\\" from \\\"neither is right.\\\" This is a genuine, working precedent, but it categorizes *what kind*\\nof drift occurred, not *which side should change* \u2014 item #3054's Deliverable 4 explicitly requires\\nthe latter (three adjudication outcomes: doc correct/impl must change; impl correct/doc must\\nchange; neither correct/new direction supplied). Precedent 1's shape needs an added adjudication\\naxis to satisfy Deliverable 4 as written, not to be discarded.\\n\\n**Precedent 2 (heavier, plugin-wide, closer fit): `docs/audits/purpose-audit-*.yaml` three-file\\npipeline** (`purpose-audit-reference-manifest.yaml`, `purpose-audit-clause-evidence-manifest.yaml`,\\n`purpose-audit-remediation-plan.yaml`), auditing `docs/PURPOSE.md` against the whole plugin at\\nbaseline commits `2a58fda0`/`2c76f47d`, all marked `frozen: true`. Structure:\\n\\n1. **Reference-selection manifest**: declares a `source_precedence` order (target contract clauses\\n   \u2192 current-boundary clauses \u2192 executable protocols/config/transport/provider implementations \u2192\\n   published plugin/lifecycle/skill contracts \u2192 focused tests \u2192 supporting ADRs/procedures), an\\n   explicit include/exclude selection rule, and an `audit_safety` block (`mode: read-only`,\\n   isolated backend defaults) \u2014 this fixes what counts as evidence and how the audit avoids\\n   mutating real state before any finding is produced.\\n2. **Clause/evidence manifest**: atomically maps every clause of the target doc to selected\\n   sources, with an explicit `evidence_schema` classifying each assertion as `documented-claim`\\n   (what the system declares) / `implementation-fact` (a structural property observable in\\n   committed source) / `test-evidence` (an executable test's assertion) / `confidence`. This is\\n   directly a per-claim fact-check method matching Deliverable 4's per-discrepancy record\\n   requirement.\\n3. **Remediation plan**: dependency-ordered tasks derived from the partitioned (A-D) audit\\n   findings, with a `decision_log` of resolved user decisions (each entry: id, date, clause_ids,\\n   origin) that closes a \\\"needs user decision\\\" flag raised by a partition auditor \u2014 this is\\n   functionally the three-outcome adjudication mechanism Deliverable 4 asks for, already\\n   implemented and used at plugin scale (not package scale) in this repo.\\n\\nPrecedent 2 is the closer methodological fit for #3054's Deliverable 4 scope (whole plugin,\\nadjudicated outcomes) than Precedent 1 (package-scoped, drift-type-only). A related process audit,\\n`docs/process-audit-backlog-lifecycle-2026-03-02.md` (2026-03-02, method: `/improve-processes`\\nexcellence checklist + triage protocol), is a third precedent but targets workflow-process quality\\nagainst a checklist rubric, not document-vs-code fact-checking \u2014 relevant to the \\\"workflow\\\" leg of\\nDeliverable 4's \\\"code, workflow, and prose instruction alike\\\" but not a drift-audit method on its\\nown.\\n\\n### Ecosystem and Compatibility \u2014 Gaps (tool unavailability)\\n\\nNo `WebSearch`, `Ref`, or `context7` MCP tools were present in this agent's available toolset for\\nthis task, despite being named in the loaded api-state/ecosystem-research skill instructions. Per\\nthis repo's own operating expectation (\\\"technology may name an internal module... external angles\\nwill report short Gaps sections, which is expected\\\"), no external OSS convention survey (e.g. how\\nother multi-plugin/monorepo agent-tooling projects structure per-module docs) was performed. The\\nonly external input available \u2014 the installed `mattpocock-skills:domain-modeling` skill's own\\nSKILL.md \u2014 was read directly and is cited in full above; treat it as the entire \\\"ecosystem\\\" input\\nto this note, not a survey.\\n\\n### Conflicts\\n\\n[CONFLICT] RT-ICA condition 3 on this item's own body states: \\\"CONTEXT.md + root CONTEXT-MAP.md,\\nadded this session.\\\" Verified state at research time: `CONTEXT-MAP.md` is absent from `main` and\\nexists only committed on open PR #3080's unmerged branch (`docs/markdown-engine-decisions`, commit\\n`9bcc2bbf0`); it is placed at the monorepo root, not the plugin root \u2014 a distinction the RT-ICA\\nsnapshot's phrasing does not disambiguate.\\n[RESOLVED \u2014 this is a single verifiable fact, not a disagreement between independent research\\nangles]: `CONTEXT-MAP.md` is committed on PR #3080's branch only (absent from `main`), at path\\n`/CONTEXT-MAP.md` (repo root), containing exactly one context entry pointing to\\n`./plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md`. This is itself a live instance of the documentation\\ndrift #3054 targets \u2014 RT-ICA condition 3's wording should be corrected at the next grooming pass\\nto state the file is unmerged (on PR #3080) and mis-rooted, not \\\"added this session\\\" without\\nqualification.\\n\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\n\\nNone applicable \u2014 this was a single-agent pass with no independent second angle to cross-check\\nagainst (tool unavailability, see Ecosystem section above).\\n\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\n\\n- No external convention survey beyond the one locally-installed skill (`WebSearch`/`Ref`/\\n  `context7` unavailable in this session's toolset).\\n- Runtime/dynamic drift (behaviour only observable by executing code \u2014 e.g. actual FastMCP tool\\n  response shapes vs. documented schemas) was not measured; this note is static-analysis only,\\n  consistent with the codebase-auditor angle's operating constraints.\\n- The 244 `.md` files in the plugin were not individually checked for drift status \u2014 that\\n  execution is Deliverable 4 itself, out of scope for this research pass, which targets RT-ICA\\n  condition 4 (shared root path / naming convention) and drift-audit methodology only.\\n- The `docs/audits/purpose-audit-*.yaml` pipeline's completion/adoption status beyond its frozen\\n  baseline headers (dated 2026-07-26, commits `2a58fda0`/`2c76f47d`) was not traced further \u2014\\n  worth a direct read of `purpose-audit-remediation-plan.yaml`'s full `decision_log` and task list\\n  before Deliverable 4 execution begins, to confirm whether its remediation tasks were carried out\\n  and whether its manifest schema is reusable as-is or needs adaptation for a code-vs-doc (not\\n  purpose-doc-vs-code) audit target.\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Every code package in the plugin has an architecture description covering role, owns, consumes, consumed-by, and detail location\\n- [ ] Near-collisions between modules and concepts carry explicit distinction notes\\n- [ ] Concepts and glossary consolidated to one definition each, with other references linking rather than restating\\n- [ ] Shared root path and naming convention defined and documented\\n- [ ] Drift audit complete: every plugin document fact-checked against code, workflow, and prose behaviour\\n- [ ] Drift review chart produced, supporting all three adjudication outcomes per entry, and reviewed\\n- [ ] Cross-reference table produced identifying every multi-document topic and its duplicate sources\\n- [ ] The resulting material is reachable from the plugin's discovery index, not orphaned\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs **an agent or contributor working anywhere in the development-harness plugin**, I want **an authoritative per-module architecture description, a consolidated concept glossary, and a verified record of where documentation and behaviour disagree** so that **I can find the right component and the right detail without inferring the system's shape from whichever file I happened to open first**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation, 2026-08-20 \u2014 architecture drift investigation across the backlog and markdown-handling subsystems\\n- **Priority**: P1 \u2014 documentation drift is actively producing incorrect agent work, with repeated instances observed in a single session\\n- **Sequencing**: Deferred. Begins after the targeted markdown-consumption architecture description is settled; that smaller surface serves as the pattern for this broader pass.\\n- **Related**: #3050 (ADR identifiers referenced but undefined \u2014 a subset of the same problem); #3052 (tool documentation omits implemented parameters); PR #3053 (targeted integration of three overlapping documents, done reactively rather than systematically)\\n\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Architecture Baseline\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nIntended behaviour and component ownership are recorded in two documents:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` \u2014 normative requirements R1\u2013R8 and the required flow\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md` \u2014 package map, ownership boundaries, and do-not-confuse-with notes\\n\\nBoth are under review in PR #3053 (integration and deduplication against existing docs) and PR #3056 (component map, contract scope binding, review-round amendments).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Sequencing\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n#3057 requires the grooming validation gate to read section presence from the engine before the hand-built structures it currently depends on are removed.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Gap Items Bridging Current State To The Architecture\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n| Item | Gap |\\n|---|---|\\n| #3057 | Default item-read path hand-builds its index and pagination instead of using the engine |\\n| #3058 | Plan, task, and artifact reads are unbounded against the harness response cap |\\n| #3059 | Navigation and pagination are reimplemented on top of the engine rather than used from it |\\n| #3060 | Runtime hints steer agents toward full-content retrieval |\\n| #3061 | Sections and artifacts are separate lookups rather than one inventory |\\n| #3062 | Paginated content has no content identity, so pages re-collect and can span versions |\\n| #3063 | The CLI cannot reach address-based navigation |\\n| #3064 | Agent instructions mandate full-item fetches the contract supersedes |\\n\\nRelated, filed separately: #3055 (registered artifacts whose content cannot be read) blocks #3061; #3050 (referenced ADRs with no definition documents); #2453 (agents hardcode section names with no route to a source of truth).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Required Deliverables\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### 1. Per-module architecture and relationships\\n\\nFor every code package in the plugin, a description covering: the module's role in one sentence; what it owns; what it consumes and from where; what consumes it; and where its authoritative detail lives.\\n\\nEach near-collision between modules or concepts carries an explicit distinction note stating that the two are not the same and how to tell them apart. The observed failure mode is not \\\"could not find it\\\" but \\\"found something adjacent and assumed it was the thing\\\" \u2014 the distinction notes exist to stop that specifically.\\n\\n### 2. Authoritative concept consolidation and glossary\\n\\nOne definition per concept, stated once, in a known location. Every other document referencing that concept links to the definition rather than restating it.\\n\\n### 3. Structure design at a shared root path\\n\\nA defined home for this material, with the arrangement and naming convention documented, so that a new module's documentation has an obvious correct location and an agent has an obvious correct place to look.\\n\\n### 4. Documentation-versus-behaviour drift audit\\n\\nEvery existing document in the plugin is fact-checked against what the plugin actually does \u2014 code, workflow, and prose instruction alike. For each discrepancy found, record: the document and the specific claim; the actual observed behaviour; and the nature of the difference.\\n\\nThe output is a review chart listing every discrepancy for adjudication. Each entry must support three outcomes:\\n\\n- the documentation is correct and the implementation must change;\\n- the implementation is correct and the documentation must change;\\n- neither matches the desired architecture, and a written direction is supplied for what the correct state is.\\n\\nThe chart is produced for review. Entries are not resolved unilaterally.\\n\\n### 5. Cross-reference and duplicate-source table\\n\\nA table identifying every topic described in more than one document, showing which documents cover it and where they agree, diverge, or contradict. This is the deduplication work list: each row resolves to one authoritative source with the others reduced to links.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"787cef9d4534b508bda17943a22f398d1fd4a49a774a5292bfc8037805f032d9\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPwH2Mg\"}"}